  "_rate_sources": {
    "_about": "Every piece_rate row above is cited here, against the source that states it. The self-test refuses a row with no citation, and refuses a citation that does not contain the rate it is meant to support — so raising 100 to 120 without re-reading the source fails a test rather than reaching a payslip. Piece-rate people with no row are listed in _no_rate_stated below.",
    "meerachauhan": "100 per hour. Stated four times and never contradicted: the §3.3 rate table row ‘meerachauhan | hours × ₹100 | 3.3’; the §3.3 locked formula ‘meerachauhan wage = Staff Report hours × Rs 100/hr (no monthly salary, no attendance tracking)’; the Part IV sheet formula ‘# meerachauhan wage = total meerachauhan hours × 100’; and the acceptance checklist item ‘meerachauhan wages = hours × Rs 100/hr (no attendance row)’."
  },

  "_no_rate_stated": {
    "_about": "Piece-rate people the source names without ever stating a rate. They keep their pay_basis and deliberately have no piece_rate row, so a month that needs one reports Unresolvable instead of earning a guessed figure. This is not permission to pay zero — nothing posts.",
    "ishitasompura": "Four mentions, no rate in any of them: the Book 1 staff table ‘ishitasompura | M | Muslim | — | — | Contract: Piece Rate’; the identical Book 2 §2.1 roster row; the §3.3 locked formula ‘ishitasompura Wage = Piece Rate, no attendance’; and the Power BI staff master row ‘ishitasompura | Iron (Piece rate)’. This file previously carried 100 per_hour for him — the same figure as the other piece-rate contractor, which is where it appears to have been copied from. It is gone."
  },

